`inferAgent(values.model)` throws a raw `Error` when the model is unrecognised, and this happens *before* `argsSchema.safeParse` runs. A user who passes `-m unknown-model` gets an unhandled exception with a stack trace instead of the clean, formatted error output set up at lines 102–105. Wrap the call in a try/catch and route the error through the same `console.error` + `process.exit(1)` path:

```ts
let agent: string;
try {
  agent = values.agent ?? (values.model ? inferAgent(values.model) : 'claude');
} catch (err) {
  console.error(pc.red(err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)));
  process.exit(1);
}
```

Both branches of this ternary are textually identical, which will make every future reader suspect it is a bug or dead code. The ternary is actually necessary—TypeScript uses it to narrow `args.agent`, `args.model`, and `args.effort` to the correct discriminated-union variant so the assignment to `AgentVariant` type-checks without a cast. Add a brief inline comment to make the intent clear:

```ts
// Ternary is required: TypeScript narrows each branch to the correct AgentVariant discriminant
const variant: AgentVariant =
  args.agent === 'claude'
    ? { agent: args.agent, model: args.model, effort: args.effort }
    : { agent: args.agent, model: args.model, effort: args.effort };
```

This `catch` block silently swallows any error from the `git rev-parse` calls with only an inline comment. The comment says `/* not in a git repo */`, but any other git failure (e.g. a corrupted `.git`, a permission error) would also be silently absorbed and produce misleading `'unknown'` values in `environment.json`. Per project convention, even non-critical catch blocks must log the error at minimum at debug level rather than discarding it entirely:

```ts
} catch (err) {
  // Not in a git repo or git unavailable — environment fields will be 'unknown'
  logger?.log?.(`captureEnvironment: git unavailable: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`);
}
```
(Or log unconditionally to stderr if no logger is available at this scope.)

All five `vi.mock()` calls in this file (lines 10, 13, 16, 27, 30) are missing the `{ spy: true }` option required by the project's Vitest mock-lifecycle convention. Per the project rule, this applies to factory-style mocks as well as simple ones. Add the option as the third argument to each call:

```ts
vi.mock('./prepare-trial', () => ({
  prepareTrial: vi.fn(),
}), { spy: true });
```
